    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" (which enables an interface) would be to NOT have the \"disable\" statement. However, since the original command is \"no shutdown\" (enable), the Juniper equivalent would be to either omit the \"disable\" statement or explicitly remove it with \"delete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\". \\n\\nIf you want the positive enabling statement (closer to the \"no shutdown\" spirit), it would be:\\n##\\ndelete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nFor most practical purposes in Juniper, interfaces are enabled by default, so you would typically just omit any disabling command rather than explicitly enabling it.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" (which enables an interface) would be to NOT have the \"disable\" statement. However, since the original command is \"no shutdown\" (enable), the Juniper equivalent would be to either omit the \"disable\" statement or explicitly remove it with \"delete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\". \\n\\nIf you want the positive enabling statement (closer to the \"no shutdown\" spirit), it would be:\\n##\\ndelete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nFor most practical purposes in Juniper, interfaces are enabled by default, so you would typically just omit any disabling command rather than explicitly enabling it.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable'}]]": 1,
